Author: <a href="https://www.smspower.org:443/forums/profile.php?mode=viewprofile&u=7980" target="_blank">Centrale</a><br />
	Posted: Thu Oct 29, 2026 7:22 pm (GMT 0)<br /><br /><span class="postbody">
	<blockquote>  <div class="attribution">Bock wrote    <script type="text/javascript" src="./templates/select_expand_bbcodes.js"></script>    <script type="text/javascript">    
<!--
        var id = 'SXBB' + (1000 + Math.floor(Math.random() * 5000));        SXBB[id] = new _SXBB(id);        SXBB[id].T['select'] = 'Select';        SXBB[id].T['expand'] = '[+]';        SXBB[id].T['contract'] = '[-]';        SXBB[id].writeCmd();    
//-->
    </script>  </div>  <div class="body">    <script type="text/javascript">      
<!--
        SXBB[id].writeDiv();      
//-->
    </script>The general idea is likely not to prevent you or anyone from posting things, but to be considerate that several people are losing motivation and interest in their work and the community given the amount of stuff released, particularly the amount of half-finished stuff.    <script type="text/javascript">      
<!--
        document.write('</div>');      
//-->
    </script>  </div></blockquote>
<br />

<br />
Motivation must be intrinsic, now more than ever. External validation is nice to have and helpful, but this matter of a skillful and relatively rare ability being augmented/replaced by automation has repeated over and over throughout history. The people who appreciate and (at least partially) understand the effort and skill that goes into coding still remain appreciative and should continue to be encouraging. Not to mention the personal satisfaction that comes from a job well done. That's why there are still calligraphers, drummers, etc.</span><br />
